        <div id="iso100-vs-elite" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. ISO100 vs Elite 100% Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the decision most Dymatize shoppers face, so it deserves its own section. Both powders give about 25 g of protein. The difference is that ISO100 is all isolate, partly hydrolyzed, while Elite Whey blends concentrate with isolate and peptides. That changes three things: lactose, calories and price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lactose is the big one. Whey isolate is filtered to remove most lactose and fat, so ISO100 usually sits under 1 g of sugar per scoop in plain flavors. Concentrate carries more lactose. If dairy gives you bloating or cramps, ISO100 is often the difference between a shake you tolerate and one you do not. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey versus whey isolate</a> guide explains the filtering.
          </p>

          <p>
            Calories are a smaller gap than people think. ISO100 runs about 110 to 120 calories per scoop and Elite Whey about 140, so you save 20 to 30 calories per shake. That matters on a strict cut, but for most people it is noise next to the rest of the day's food.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydrolysis is the least important difference. Partly pre-digesting whey makes it absorb slightly faster, but research has not shown that this produces more muscle over time than regular whey when total protein is matched. Pay for ISO100 for the lactose and macros, not the hydrolysis claim. If neither applies to you, Elite Whey is the smarter buy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Lactose problems or a tight cut? ISO100. Neither? Elite Whey.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The protein dose is the same. You are paying for lower lactose and roughly 20 to 30 fewer calories per scoop.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

