        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Informed Choice Testing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dymatize powders carry the Informed Choice logo, per retailer listings for ISO100, Elite 100% Whey and Elite Casein at the time of writing. Informed Choice is run by LGC, a sports anti-doping laboratory, and certified products are regularly tested for substances banned in sport. Dymatize also says it manufactures in GMP and sport-certified facilities.
          </p>

          <p>
            Informed Choice is the program behind the better-known Informed Sport, and the main difference is how often batches are tested. For most recreational lifters, Informed Choice is plenty. Elite athletes in strict anti-doping systems may prefer Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport, which test every batch before release.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification status is set per product and can change, so a drug-tested athlete should look up the exact product and flavor on the certifier's website before competing. Do not assume a new limited-edition flavor carries the same status as the original.
          </p>

          <p>
            Banned-substance testing is not the same as testing for heavy metals or an ingredient-quality audit. Whey generally tests lower for heavy metals than plant proteins, but the Informed Choice logo tells you about doping risk, not about sweeteners or additives. For the full picture on safety, read our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flavors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Flavors, Sweeteners and Mixing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flavor is a big part of why Dymatize sells. ISO100 comes in Gourmet Chocolate, Gourmet Vanilla, Fudge Brownie, Chocolate Peanut Butter, the Pebbles flavors, the Dunkin flavors and more at the time of writing. Buyer feedback on ISO100 is generally strong, especially for chocolate flavors and the cereal flavors.
          </p>

          <p>
            On sweeteners, the ISO100 Gourmet Chocolate ingredient list we found includes sucralose and steviol glycosides, along with soy lecithin, salt and natural and artificial flavors. Other flavors vary. If you want to avoid sucralose entirely, Dymatize is not the brand for you; an unflavored isolate or a stevia-only brand is a better fit. Our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">best unflavored protein powder</a> guide has options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixability is where ISO100 really shines. Isolates dissolve thinner and cleaner than concentrates, and buyers consistently describe ISO100 as mixing with a few shakes in water with little foam or clumping. Elite Whey is a little thicker and creamier, which some people prefer in milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Elite Casein behaves differently. It thickens with less liquid, which makes it good for pudding, overnight oats and mug cakes. In a shaker with water, buyers say it can be thick and benefits from extra liquid. If you plan to bake or make dessert-style protein, casein is the best Dymatize product for the job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip Dymatize if you avoid artificial sweeteners.</strong> The flavored range relies on sucralose. Choose an unflavored or stevia-sweetened powder instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip it if you are vegan or have a milk allergy.</strong> Every product in this review is dairy-based and carries a milk allergen warning, and some flavors also list soy lecithin. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide covers dairy-free options.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip ISO100 if you tolerate dairy and want value.</strong> You would be paying an isolate premium for a benefit you do not need. Elite Whey, or a cheaper mass-market whey, gets you the same 25 g for less.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip Super Mass Gainer unless you are truly underweight or struggle to eat.</strong> For most lifters, adding 1,200 calories of mostly maltodextrin will put on fat as well as muscle. A regular whey plus extra food usually does the job better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Matching the Tub to Your Goal
          </h2>

          <p>
            For building muscle, most adults do well on roughly 1.6 g of protein per kg of body weight per day, split across meals. One 25 g scoop of ISO100 or Elite Whey after training covers one of those meals. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> works out your daily target.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cutting, ISO100 makes most sense. It gives the most protein per calorie in the range and is the least likely to cause digestive trouble. For a lean bulk or general fitness, Elite Whey does the same job for less. For a bedtime shake, Elite Casein's slower digestion is the reason to buy it, though total daily protein still matters more than timing.
          </p>

          <p>
            For gaining weight, start with Elite Whey mixed in whole milk with oats and peanut butter. Move to Super Mass Gainer only if that is still not enough calories. Many people find half a serving of a gainer is plenty.
          </p>

          <p>
            A shake is a supplement, not a replacement for meals. Readers with kidney disease, those who are pregnant, and anyone on medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a high-protein supplement. This review is label analysis, not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

